<!--
  The app's landing page (everything that finishes an onboarding/pairing/flash
  flow navigates back here with clearHistory), and the host for all three
  bodies the phone can show without the user navigating anywhere.

  ROUND 3 SWAPPED WHAT THIS LANDS ON. It used to open on faceclaw's own device
  hub — connection card, recent conversations, glasses display controls, a
  "Review" section — with the purpose-built Exocortex app list buried three
  levels inside it (here -> Settings -> Apps). Chris, using the real build:
  "Exocortex" should land on the app list; the hub content "isn't wrong, it's
  just mislabeled as the *default* view." So the app list is the home body now
  and the hub moved to phone-ui/settings-hub-page, one tap away via the gear in
  the header.

  THE PAGE STAYS THE LANDING PAGE. The swap is in what the bodies show, not in
  where boot points: this page is the only thing that hosts the cover glance,
  the per-app companions, the "Attention needed" modal and the glasses-driven
  text editor, and it is the clearHistory target of six flows. Pointing boot at
  a different module would have taken all of that off the phone's resting
  screen to move one list.

  THREE BODIES, ONE PAGE. Neither switch is a navigation the user performs:

    cover-glance      the Fold is shut. One screenful: connection, what is on
                      the lens, nothing to scroll. Unfolding is the navigation.
    a companion       the Fold is open and an app with a companion view is the
                      foreground app ON THE GLASSES. Ghost is the only one
                      built. The phone becomes that session's second view.
    the app list      everything else — and the body "Exocortex" returns to.

  They share the page's grid cell rather than being separate pages: a page swap
  would leave a back-stack entry behind a screen nobody asked to leave, and the
  warnings modal and the glasses' keyboard-input editor have to float over all
  three regardless. MainViewModel owns the choice; see its "Which body the page
  is showing" section.

  NO ACTION BAR. Round 2 hid faceclaw's ActionBar while a companion was up but
  kept it over the hub, because the hub needed its overflow menu. The hub is
  not here any more — every body this page can show is an Exocortex screen, so
  phone-ui/exocortex-header is the whole of this page's chrome and the stale
  hardcoded "Faceclaw" title went with the bar. The overflow menu moved intact
  to settings-hub-page, which is the surface those items were always about.
-->
<Page xmlns="http://schemas.nativescript.org/tns.xsd" xmlns:ui="phone-ui"
    navigatingTo="navigatingTo" loaded="loaded" unloaded="unloaded">

    <GridLayout>
        <!-- Fold SHUT: the cover screen gets a glance, not the app. It is
             deliberately bare — one screenful by design, and it already names
             the connection and the foreground app that the header would
             otherwise repeat. -->
        <ui:cover-glance visibility="{{ coverGlanceVisibility }}" />

        <!-- Fold OPEN. ONE HEADER, THEN A BODY. exocortex-header is the single
             row of chrome every Exocortex screen on this page shares
             (Exocortex — app — connection — warnings — settings), and round 3
             extended it from the companion to the home body as well, because
             once the ActionBar was gone it was the only chrome left.

             The inner cell holds the bodies. Per-app companions come first;
             the app list is the fallback, which is what makes it the thing you
             land on. The generalized "which view per app" mechanism is still
             its own queued design job; this is only the frame it hangs in.

             The header binds to MainViewModel (inherited); each companion body
             overrides bindingContext with its own view model. -->
        <GridLayout id="exocortexChrome" rows="auto,*" visibility="{{ exocortexChromeVisibility }}">
            <ui:exocortex-header row="0" />
            <GridLayout row="1">
                <ui:ghost-companion visibility="{{ ghostCompanionVisibility }}"
                    bindingContext="{{ ghostCompanion }}" />

                <!-- THE HOME BODY: the app list, checkbox + reorder +
                     tap-to-launch. Same component the standalone
                     exocortex-apps-page shows, bound to MainViewModel's own
                     ExocortexAppsViewModel. The two rows above it are pinned
                     rather than scrolled: both are transient states you want
                     to act on immediately, not list content. -->
                <GridLayout rows="auto,auto,*" visibility="{{ homeBodyVisibility }}">
                    <!-- Only while looking at the app list with Ghost still
                         focused over there; the way back to the companion. -->
                    <StackLayout row="0" class="home-row" tap="{{ onShowGhostTap }}" visibility="{{ ghostReturnRowVisibility }}">
                        <Label text="Ghost" class="home-row-title" />
                        <Label text="Back to the companion for the session on the glasses" class="home-row-meta" textWrap="true" />
                    </StackLayout>

                    <!-- Screen recording is started from settings-hub-page's
                         overflow menu, but it keeps running once you come back
                         here, so the stop button has to be on this screen too.
                         Collapsed unless a recording is actually running. -->
                    <Button row="1" text="Stop recording" tap="{{ onStopRecordingTap }}" visibility="{{ stopRecordingButtonVisibility }}" />

                    <ui:exocortex-apps-body row="2" bindingContext="{{ appList }}" />
                </GridLayout>
            </GridLayout>
        </GridLayout>

        <!-- "Attention needed". Opened by the warning triangle, which lives in
             exocortex-header now that this page has no ActionBar to carry it.
             It stays on this page alone (settings-hub-page and the mirror page
             deliberately do not repeat it) because it is an app-level concern
             and this is the screen the phone rests on. -->
        <ScrollView
            class="warnings-modal"
            visibility="{{ warningsModalVisibility }}"
            width="{{ warningsModalWidth }}"
            horizontalAlignment="center"
            verticalAlignment="center">
            <StackLayout class="warnings-modal-content">
                <Label text="Attention needed" class="h2" />
                <StackLayout visibility="{{ evenAppConflictWarningVisibility }}" class="warnings-modal-item">
                    <Label text="{{ evenAppConflictMessage }}" textWrap="true" />
                    <Button text="Open Even app settings" tap="{{ onOpenEvenAppSettingsTap }}" class="-primary" />
                </StackLayout>
                <StackLayout visibility="{{ firmwareWarningVisibility }}" class="warnings-modal-item">
                    <Label text="{{ firmwareWarningMessage }}" textWrap="true" />
                    <Button text="Install custom firmware" tap="{{ onInstallFirmwareTap }}" class="-primary" />
                </StackLayout>
                <StackLayout visibility="{{ batteryOptimizationWarningVisibility }}" class="warnings-modal-item">
                    <Label text="Android will slow Faceclaw to a crawl when the phone screen is off unless it is exempt from battery optimization." textWrap="true" />
                    <Button text="Allow unrestricted background usage" tap="{{ onAllowBackgroundUsageTap }}" class="-primary" />
                </StackLayout>
                <Button text="Close" tap="{{ onWarningsModalCloseTap }}" />
            </StackLayout>
        </ScrollView>
        <!-- Keyboard-input mode: the glasses navigated into a text setting and
             the phone is the keyboard. Anchored to the bottom of the (keyboard-
             shrunk) page. The mirror page and settings-hub-page carry identical
             copies, because the glasses can ask for text while any of the three
             is on screen. -->
        <StackLayout
            class="system-name-modal"
            visibility="{{ textSettingEditorVisibility }}"
            horizontalAlignment="stretch"
            verticalAlignment="bottom">
            <Label text="{{ activeTextEditorTitle }}" class="h2" />
            <!-- Per-field labels only matter with two fields; a single-field
                 edit's field title is the same string as the editor title. -->
            <Label text="{{ activeTextSettingTitle }}" visibility="{{ secondaryTextSettingVisibility }}" />
            <TextField
                id="settingsTextField"
                text="{{ activeTextSettingValue }}"
                textChange="{{ onTextSettingTextChange }}"
                returnPress="{{ onPrimaryTextSettingReturnPress }}"
                autocorrect="false"
                autocapitalizationType="none"
                keyboardType="{{ activeTextSettingKeyboardType }}"
                secure="{{ activeTextSettingSecure }}"
                returnKeyType="{{ primaryTextSettingReturnKeyType }}" />
            <StackLayout visibility="{{ secondaryTextSettingVisibility }}">
                <Label text="{{ secondaryTextSettingTitle }}" />
                <TextField
                    id="secondarySettingsTextField"
                    text="{{ secondaryTextSettingValue }}"
                    textChange="{{ onSecondaryTextSettingTextChange }}"
                    returnPress="{{ onSecondaryTextSettingReturnPress }}"
                    autocorrect="false"
                    autocapitalizationType="none"
                    keyboardType="{{ secondaryTextSettingKeyboardType }}"
                    secure="{{ secondaryTextSettingSecure }}"
                    returnKeyType="done" />
            </StackLayout>
        </StackLayout>
    </GridLayout>
</Page>
